    <h1>Digital Footprint Defender</h1>
    <p class="lesson-subtitle">
      In this mission, you will learn how your online choices can leave a trail and how to post in a way that protects your safety and reputation.
    </p>

    <section class="lesson-card">
      <h2>Mission Goal</h2>
      <p>
        A digital footprint is the trail of information you leave behind when you use the internet. This can include posts, comments, photos, likes, searches, messages, and accounts.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some parts of your digital footprint are created by you. Other parts may be created when people tag you, screenshot something, or share your information.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-card">
      <h2>Key Vocabulary</h2>

      <div class="vocab-box">
        <strong>Digital Footprint:</strong>
        The trail of information you leave online.
      </div>

      <div class="vocab-box">
        <strong>Post:</strong>
        Something you share online, such as a picture, video, comment, or message.
      </div>

      <div class="vocab-box">
        <strong>Reputation:</strong>
        What others may think about you based on your actions and choices.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-card">
      <h2>What Creates a Digital Footprint?</h2>

      <p>
        Your digital footprint can grow every time you use technology. Even small actions can leave information behind.
      </p>

      <h3>Examples include:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Posting a photo</li>
        <li>Commenting on a video</li>
        <li>Liking a post</li>
        <li>Joining an online group</li>
        <li>Sharing your location</li>
        <li>Using a school account</li>
        <li>Sending messages</li>
        <li>Being tagged in someone else’s post</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-card">
      <h2>Why Your Digital Footprint Matters</h2>

      <p>
        Your online choices can affect how people see you. Friends, family, teachers, coaches, clubs, scholarship groups, and future employers may see parts of your digital footprint.
      </p>

      <p>
        Even if you delete something, someone may have already saved it, screenshotted it, or shared it.
      </p>

      <h3>Before posting, ask:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Would I be okay with a parent, teacher, or coach seeing this?</li>
        <li>Could this hurt someone’s feelings?</li>
        <li>Does this reveal private information?</li>
        <li>Could this be misunderstood?</li>
        <li>Am I posting because I am angry or upset?</li>
        <li>Would future me be proud of this?</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-card">
      <h2>Acceptable vs. Risky Posting</h2>

      <h3>Usually acceptable:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Sharing a school project without private details</li>
        <li>Posting a hobby or achievement</li>
        <li>Encouraging a friend</li>
        <li>Sharing creative work</li>
        <li>Posting after checking the background of the photo</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Risky posting:</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Posting someone else without permission</li>
        <li>Sharing your location in real time</li>
        <li>Posting when angry</li>
        <li>Sharing rumors or embarrassing photos</li>
        <li>Posting personal information</li>
        <li>Using hurtful or threatening language</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-card">
      <h2>Real-Life Example</h2>

      <div class="scenario-box">
        <p>
          A student records a classmate falling in the hallway and posts it because they think it is funny. Other students start sharing it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        This may seem like a joke, but it can embarrass the classmate and become part of everyone’s digital footprint. The person who posted it may also get in trouble for sharing something hurtful.
      </p>

      <h3>Better choice:</h3>
      <p>
        Help the person, check if they are okay, and do not post the video.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-card">
      <h2>Mini Activity: Post or Pause?</h2>
      <p>
        Decide whether each situation is okay to post or whether you should pause.
      </p>

      <div class="activity-box">
        <ol>
          <li>A picture of your art project with no personal information showing.</li>
          <li>A video of someone crying after an argument.</li>
          <li>A selfie that shows your house number in the background.</li>
          <li>A congratulation post for your friend after they gave permission.</li>
          <li>An angry comment written right after someone upset you.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <p>
        A Digital Footprint Defender pauses before posting.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-card">
      <h2>Quick Check</h2>

      <div class="quick-check">
        <p><strong>Question 1:</strong> What is a digital footprint?</p>
        <p><strong>Question 2:</strong> Why can deleting a post not always erase the problem?</p>
        <p><strong>Question 3:</strong> Name one question you should ask before posting.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-card">
      <h2>Mission Reminder from Meme</h2>
      <p>
        Meme says: “Your posts leave footprints. Make sure they lead somewhere safe and kind.”
      </p>
    </section>

